 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 544515,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": " Use  Figure 12.177 to find a Hamilton path between vertices <i>C</i> and <i>E</i>. ",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>A path that visits each vertex exactly once is a Hamilton path. Recall that a path is a walk that has no repeated vertices or edges. It does not need to start and end on the same vertex.</p>\n<p>Since you start at <i>C</i>, <i>A</i> must be next. So far you have:</p>\n<p> <i>C → A</i></p>\n<p>If you then went to <i>F</i>, you would have no way to get to <i>B</i> without visiting <i>F</i> twice. So, you must go from <i>A</i> to <i>B</i>. Then from <i>B</i> to <i>F</i>.</p>\n<p><i>C → A → B → F</i></p>\n<p>You need to end at <i>E</i>, so visit <i>D</i> next.</p>\n<p><i>C → A → B → F → D</i></p>\n<p>Finally, end at <i>E</i>.</p>\n<p><i>C → A → B → F → D → E</i></p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<i>C</i> → <i>A</i> → <i>B</i> → <i>F</i> → <i>D</i> → <i>E</i>"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
